course: dictionary
lesson: 13752
title: Interlock
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To interlock means to fit or lock firmly together, with parts of one thing going into gaps in another. Think of jigsaw pieces: each one has a shape that slides into a gap in the next.
explain: «The two halves of the wooden box interlock without any nails.» The pieces fit into each other, so the box stays strong. Interlock is a verb, so you can change it to interlocks, interlocked or interlocking.
explain: Interlock can also mean that one thing depends on another. «Our plans interlock: we can only start the garden once the shed is built.» When you meet interlock, ask yourself: do the parts fit into each other, or does one thing rely on another?
fact: Interlock means to fit or lock firmly together.
fact: «Her fingers interlocked as she held her friend's hand.» Interlocked fingers show that two people are close and holding tight.
fact: «The cogs in the clock interlock to keep the hands moving.» Interlocking gears and cogs turn each other.
fact: In design and technology, interlocking joints hold wood together without nails.
fact: As a noun, an interlock is a device whose parts are linked together, such as a safety lock on a machine.
exercise: «The two pieces of the puzzle interlock perfectly.» What does interlock mean here? | To lose a piece | *To fit firmly together | To break into small pieces | To paint in bright colours
exercise: «Her fingers interlocked as she held her friend's hand.» What do interlocked fingers show? | Being bored with the game | Feeling very hungry | Being cold and wet | *Holding tight and being close
exercise: Which of these is most likely to interlock? | A cup of tea | A cloud in the sky | *Two jigsaw pieces | A blade of grass
test: «The wooden shelf was held together with interlocking joints, not nails.» What does interlocking mean here? | Falling down | *Fitting together without nails | Painted in many colours | Sitting far apart
test: «Our plans interlock, so we can only start the garden once the shed is built.» What does interlock mean here? | *Closely joined, so one depends on the other | Loud and noisy | Separated and unrelated | Finished early
exam: Which word means to fit firmly together? | Dribble | Scatter | *Interlock | Wobble
